<article class="env-variant" id="05DW">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05DW">Variant <span data-tag="05DW">5.1.5.17</span>.</a> Let $U: \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a locally cartesian fibration of $\infty $-categories and let $n \geq 0$ be an integer. Then $U$ is $n$-faithful (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="05AX" href="/tag/05AX">4.8.3.12</a>) if and only if, for each object $C \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}_{C} = \{ C\}  \times _{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$ is locally $(n-2)$-truncated (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="0584" href="/tag/0584">4.7.6.1</a>). </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      We proceed as in the proof of Proposition <a data-tag="047P" href="/tag/047P">5.1.5.14</a>. Assume that, for each object $C \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}_{C}$ is locally $(n-2)$-truncated; we will show that the functor $U$ is $n$-faithful (the reverse implication follows from Proposition <a data-tag="05BD" href="/tag/05BD">4.8.3.36</a>, and does not require the assumption that $U$ is locally cartesian). By virtue of Proposition <a data-tag="05AN" href="/tag/05AN">4.8.2.25</a>, we may assume without loss of generality that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}= \Delta ^1$. Fix a pair of objects $X,Z \in \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$; we wish to show that the map of Kan complexes $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}}(X,Z) \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}( U(X), U(Z) )$ is $(n-2)$-truncated. We may assume that $U(X) \leq U(Z)$ (otherwise, both Kan complexes are empty and there is nothing to prove); in this case, we wish to show that the morphism space $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}}(X,Z)$ is $(n-1)$-truncated (Example <a data-tag="0562" href="/tag/0562">3.5.9.4</a>). If $U(X) = U(Z)$, then the desired result follows from our hypothesis on the fibers of $U$. It will therefore suffice to treat the case where $U(X) = 0$ and $U(Z) = 1$. Since $U$ is a locally cartesian fibration, we can choose a $U$-cartesian morphism $f: Y \rightarrow Z$ of $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$ satisfying $U(Y) = 0$. In this case, composition with the homotopy class $[f]$ induces a homotopy equivalence of Kan complexes $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}}( X,Y) \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}}(X, Z)$ (Corollary <a data-tag="01TQ" href="/tag/01TQ">5.1.2.3</a>). It will therefore suffice to show that the Kan complex $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}}(X,Y)$ is $(n-2)$-truncated, which follows from our assumption that the fiber $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}_{0} = U^{-1} \{ 0\} $ is locally $n$-truncated. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
